-\begin{theorem}[Complete modular invariance classification \cite{Zhu96, MooreSeiberg89}; \ClaimStatusProvedElsewhere]\label{thm:modular-classification}
-A chiral algebra $\mathcal{A}$ is modular invariant at genus 1 if and only if one of the following holds:
-
-\begin{enumerate}
-\item \emph{Critical dimension.} $c = 26$ for Virasoro (bosonic string), $c = 15$ for $N=1$ super-Virasoro (superstring), $c = 0$ for topological theories.
-\item \emph{Integer weight.} $c = 24n$ for $n \in \mathbb{Z}$ (trivial transformation).
-\item \emph{Rational CFT.} The chiral algebra has rational fusion rules and modular $S$-matrix.
-\item \emph{Orbifold.} The chiral algebra is an orbifold of a modular invariant theory.
-\end{enumerate}
-\end{theorem}
-
-\begin{proof}[Proof via representation theory]
-The classification follows from the representation theory of $SL_2(\mathbb{Z})$:
-
-\emph{Step~1.} The modular group has irreducible representations of weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}/2$.
-
-\emph{Step~2.} For weight $k = c/24$, the representation is trivial if and only if $k \in \mathbb{Z}$.
-
-\emph{Step~3.} Rational conformal field theories have finite-dimensional representation spaces, ensuring modular invariance.
-
-\emph{Step~4.} Orbifolding preserves modular invariance under appropriate conditions.
-\end{proof}
 
